  "articleBody": "If your shower is dated, leaking, or hard to clean, you face a common decision: remove and replace just the shower, or remodel the entire bathroom? Here is how to evaluate the tradeoff.\n\nShower Removal and Replacement:\n- Key differences: Focuses strictly on the wet area. The shower enclosure, pan, valve, and surrounding backer board come out, while existing vanities, toilets, and floor tiles remain untouched.\n- When to choose: Ideal when the rest of your bathroom layout works well, finishes are in good shape, and your primary goal is fixing a leak or modernizing the shower stall.\n- Timeline and impact: Typically completed in a shorter window with minimal disruption to the rest of the room.\n\nFull Bathroom Renovation:\n- Key differences: Involves gutting the entire space down to studs and subfloor, allowing you to reconfigure plumbing, upgrade vanities, add radiant heating, and install matching floor tile.\n- When to choose: Best if your bathroom layout feels cramped, plumbing lines are aged throughout, or existing flooring and drywall show widespread wear.\n- Timeline and impact: Requires a longer construction schedule, sequential trade inspections, and temporary loss of the entire room.\n\nHow to Compare Your Options:\nBalancing cost against your long-term plans is essential. If you only need targeted shower removal and fresh tile, keeping the surrounding footprint intact saves substantial time. For honest guidance and quality craftsmanship in the Denver area, contact HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
}